The pad's button indices, which are not the ones upstream ships.

Pressing Options did not open the pause menu on hardware, and the reason turned out to
reach further than pause.

Upstream's `joystick_button_*` defaults describe an old-style SDL joystick in DualShock 3
order. This platform's SDL backend reports buttons in `SDL_GameControllerButton` order
(`oops-deps/sdl2/backend/SDL_prosperojoystick.c`): 4 is Create, 5 is the home button, 6 is
Options, 7 and 8 are the stick clicks, and the shoulders are 9 and 10.

So `start` was 8 - the right stick click - and Options did nothing. Worse, and unnoticed
until now: `l1` and `r1` were 4 and 5, which are Create and the home button, so **view
rotation had never worked at all**, and `l2`, the hold for fast rotation, was Options.

The first four are untouched. Cross, circle, square and triangle are 0..3 in both orders,
which is why the menus and the camera button worked - and why the rest went unnoticed for
as long as it did.

L2 and R2 are triggers on this pad and arrive as axes rather than buttons, so there is no
index to give them. They take the stick clicks, which Neverball binds nothing else to.

